    "evidence_text": "The disease usually does not induce heart failure but is associated with sudden death, conduction disturbances, and an increased risk of complications during anesthesia.\n\nIncremental infusion of isoproterenol induced a similar increase in HR in patients and control subjects.   \n\n\nStem cell transplantation in patients with autonomic neuropathy due to primary (AL) amyloidosis.\n\nTransplantation can be challenging due to fluid shifts, sepsis, and cardiac dysrhythmias.\n\nAmyloidosis may present with autonomic neuropathy (AN) that renders peritransplant care problematic.\n\nMedian duration of hospitalization was similar for both cohorts, as were engraftment kinetics.\n\nOn multivariate analysis, AN retained an independent adverse impact on OS.   \n\n\nAutonomic neuropathy is an independent, adverse determinant of survival in these patients.\n\nTotal atherosclerotic burden by whole body magnetic resonance angiography predicts major adverse cardiovascular events.\n\nDuring follow-up (mean 4.8 years), MACE occurred in 25 subjects (8.2%).\n\nPrognostic Value of Late Gadolinium Enhancement Cardiovascular Magnetic Resonance in Cardiac Amyloidosis\n\nCardiovascular magnetic resonance with late gadolinium enhancement (LGE) is a reference standard for the diagnosis of cardiac amyloidosis, but its potential for stratifying risk is unknown.   \n\n\nLGE was performed with phase-sensitive inversion recovery (PSIR) and without (magnitude only).\n\nThese were compared with extracellular volume measured with T1 mapping.\n\nSixty-seven patients (27%) died.\n\nCONCLUSIONS There is a continuum of cardiac involvement in systemic AL and ATTR amyloidosis.\n\nTransmural LGE is determined reliably by PSIR and represents advanced cardiac amyloidosis.\n\nHowever, less is known about its efficacy in later disease stages and in non-Val30Met mutations.\n\nWe performed a multi-center observational study on symptomatic ATTR patients prescribed to receive Tafamidis.\n\nTafamidis proved safe and well-tolerated.\n\nNeurological function worsened particularly in the first 6 months but progression slowed significantly thereafter.\n\nFifteen percent of patients showed cardiac disease progression and 30 % new onset of cardiomyopathy.\n\nA higher mBMI at baseline was associated with better preservation of neurological function.\n\nIn conclusion, neuropathy and cardiomyopathy progressed in a significant proportion of patients despite treatment.\n\nBody weight preservation is an important favorable prognostic factor.\n\nPrion-induced amyloid heart disease with high blood infectivity in transgenic mice.\n\nThe hearts of these transgenic mice contained PrPres-positive amyloid deposits that led to myocardial stiffness and cardiac disease.\n\nT1 mapping for myocardial extracellular volume measurement by CMR: bolus only versus primed infusion technique.\n\nThis bolus only approach is highly attractive, but currently limited data support its use.\n\nIn 18 subjects with severe AS the results were compared with histological CVF.   \n\n\nEvaluation of systemic amyloidosis by scintigraphy with 123I-labeled serum amyloid P component.\n\nBACKGROUND In systemic amyloidosis the distribution and progression of disease have been difficult to monitor, because they can be demonstrated only by biopsy.\n\nWe investigated whether labeled SAP could be used to locate amyloid deposits.   \n\n\nWhole-body images and regional views were obtained after 24 hours and read in a blinded fashion.   \n\n\nThe scintigraphic images obtained were characteristic and appeared to identify the extent of amyloid deposition in all 50 patients.\n\nProgressive amyloid deposition was observed in 9 of 11 patients studied serially.   \n\n\nCD11b+ monocytes abrogate Th17 CD4+ T cell-mediated experimental autoimmune myocarditis.\n\nExperimental autoimmune myocarditis (EAM) represents a Th17 T cell-mediated mouse model of postinflammatory heart disease.\n\nIn IFN-gammaR(-/-) mice, however, EAM is exacerbated and shows a chronic progressive disease course.",
